abstract:
A number of quantum Hall isospin ferromagnetic (QHIFM) states have been predicted in the ``relativistic'' zero Landau level (LL) of the graphene monolayer. However, identification of these high-field broken-symmetry states has mostly relied on macroscopic transport techniques, which lack spatial resolution. Here, we demonstrate a direct approach by imaging the QHIFM states at atomic scale with a scanning tunneling microscope. At half filling of the zero LL ($\ensuremath{
u}=0$), the system is in a spin unpolarized state and we observe a linear magnetic-field-scaling of valley splitting. The wave functions of the QHIFM states at $\ensuremath{
u}=0$ are directly imaged at the atomic scale and we observe an interaction-driven density wave featuring a Kekul'e distortion, which is responsible for the large gap in high magnetic fields. Moreover, our experiment demonstrates that both the valley and spin splittings depend on the filling factor. For example, the spin splitting in the zero LL is dramatically enhanced (in excess of about 200% at maximum) when the Fermi level lies inside spin-polarized states (at $\ensuremath{
u}=1$ or \ensuremath{-}1), accounting for strong many-body effects.

We report enhanced interlayer tunneling with reduced linewidth at zero interlayer bias in a twist-controlled double monolayer graphene heterostructure in the quantum Hall regime, when the top (${\ensuremath{
u}}_{T}$) and bottom (${\ensuremath{
u}}_{B}$) layer filling factors are near ${\ensuremath{
u}}_{T}=\ifmmode\pm\else	extpm i{}1/2,\ifmmode\pm\else	extpm i{}3/2$ and ${\ensuremath{
u}}_{B}=\ifmmode\pm\else	extpm i{}1/2,\ifmmode\pm\else	extpm i{}3/2$, and the total filling factor $\ensuremath{
u}=\ifmmode\pm\else	extpm i{}1$ or $\ifmmode\pm\else	extpm i{}3$. The zero-bias interlayer conductance peaks are stable against variations of layer filling factor, and signal the emergence of interlayer phase coherence. Our results highlight twist control as a key attribute in revealing interlayer coherence using tunneling.

We study the ballistic conductivity of bilayer graphene in the presence of symmetry-breaking terms in an effective Hamiltonian for low-energy excitations, such as the trigonal-warping term (${\ensuremath{\gamma}}_{3}$), the electron-hole symmetry-breaking interlayer hopping (${\ensuremath{\gamma}}_{4}$), and the staggered potential (${\ensuremath{\delta}}_{AB}$). Earlier, it was shown that for ${\ensuremath{\gamma}}_{3}\ensuremath{
e}0$, in the absence of remaining symmetry-breaking terms (i.e., ${\ensuremath{\gamma}}_{4}={\ensuremath{\delta}}_{AB}=0$), the conductivity ($\ensuremath{\sigma}$) approaches the value of $3{\ensuremath{\sigma}}_{0}$ for the system size $L\ensuremath{ightarrow}\ensuremath{\infty}$ [with ${\ensuremath{\sigma}}_{0}=8{e}^{2}/(\ensuremath{\pi}h)$ being the result in the absence of trigonal warping, ${\ensuremath{\gamma}}_{3}=0$]. We demonstrate that ${\ensuremath{\gamma}}_{4}\ensuremath{
e}0$ leads to the divergent conductivity ($\ensuremath{\sigma}\ensuremath{ightarrow}\ensuremath{\infty}$) if ${\ensuremath{\gamma}}_{3}\ensuremath{
e}0$, or to the vanishing conductivity ($\ensuremath{\sigma}\ensuremath{ightarrow}0$) if ${\ensuremath{\gamma}}_{3}=0$. For realistic values of the tight-binding model parameters, ${\ensuremath{\gamma}}_{3}=0.3\phantom{ule{0.16em}{0ex}}\mathrm{eV},\phantom{ule{4pt}{0ex}}{\ensuremath{\gamma}}_{4}=0.15\phantom{ule{0.16em}{0ex}}\mathrm{eV}$ (and ${\ensuremath{\delta}}_{AB}=0$), the conductivity values are in the range $\ensuremath{\sigma}/{\ensuremath{\sigma}}_{0}\ensuremath{\approx}4\ensuremath{-}5$ for $100\phantom{ule{4.pt}{0ex}}	ext{nm}<L<1\phantom{ule{0.16em}{0ex}}\ensuremath{\mu}\mathrm{m}$, in agreement with existing experimental results. The staggered potential (${\ensuremath{\delta}}_{AB}\ensuremath{
e}0$) suppresses zero-temperature transport, leading to $\ensuremath{\sigma}\ensuremath{ightarrow}0$ for $L\ensuremath{ightarrow}\ensuremath{\infty}$. Although $\ensuremath{\sigma}=\ensuremath{\sigma}(L)$ is no longer universal, the Fano factor approaches the pseudodiffusive value ($F\ensuremath{ightarrow}1/3$ for $L\ensuremath{ightarrow}\ensuremath{\infty}$) in any case with nonvanishing $\ensuremath{\sigma}$ (otherwise, $F\ensuremath{ightarrow}1$), signaling the transport is ruled by evanescent waves. Temperature effects are briefly discussed in terms of a phenomenological model for staggered potential ${\ensuremath{\delta}}_{AB}={\ensuremath{\delta}}_{AB}(T)$ showing that, for $0<T\ensuremath{\leqslant}{T}_{c}\ensuremath{\approx}12\phantom{ule{0.28em}{0ex}}\mathrm{K}$ and ${\ensuremath{\delta}}_{AB}(0)=1.5\phantom{ule{0.28em}{0ex}}\mathrm{meV},\ensuremath{\sigma}(L)$ is noticeably affected by $T$ for $L\ensuremath{\gtrsim}100$ nm.
